WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:09.120
Trick-or-treat! Fifty-six percent of&nbsp;
Americans in an online Morning Consult poll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.480
say they plan to celebrate Halloween in 2021.&nbsp;
That's up from 42 percent last year when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.120
pandemic upended people’s plans for the&nbsp;
holiday. Twenty-four percent said they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.760
plan to attend a Halloween party, with young&nbsp;
people more enthusiastic than their elders.&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.920
Parents, too, are looking forward to a night out.&nbsp;
In the latest Axios/Ipsos survey, 52 percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.120
their said taking their children trick or treating&nbsp;
represented a small risk, 19 percent a moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.360
one, and 22 percent no risk. Only 9 percent&nbsp;
said it represented a large risk, down from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.760
21 percent last year. The new responses shouldn’t&nbsp;
surprise us: After a long social drought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.280
most of us crave the kind of interactions that a&nbsp;
night out can provide, not to mention the candy.&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.720
The pandemic took a significant toll on Americans’&nbsp;
overall well being early on, but the country has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.240
been remarkably resilient in the past year and&nbsp;
a half. Most Americans are still being careful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.360
but they want to have some fun. Boo!
How do you plan to celebrate Halloween?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.440
Let us know in our poll. Also let us know&nbsp;
what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.600
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

